Coroutines whose suspension calls are all tail-calls (or have none at all) produce a single state and can never be resumed mid-body — so the `while(true) switch(gotoLabel)` dispatch machinery and related bookkeeping are unnecessary overhead. This PR addresses this in three complementary ways.

## Changes

### 1. Single-state optimization (`coroToTexpr.ml`)

When the generated state list has exactly one element, emit the state body directly as a plain void block instead of wrapping it in the `while … switch`. Any trailing `TBreak` (produced by `NextThrow`, which would normally break out of the `while…switch` to reach the error handler) is in tail position and is simply dropped — the error handler follows naturally without needing a break target.

Multi-state coroutines (those with internal gotos, e.g. a non-tail suspension followed by more work) are unaffected.

### 2. Omit `recursing` check for single-state coroutines (`coroToTexpr.ml`, `coro.ml`)

Single-state coroutines can never be resumed mid-body, which means they never recurse on themselves. The entry-point branching:

```js
if ((_hx_completion instanceof ...) && _hx_completion.recursing == false) {
    _hx_continuation = _hx_completion;
} else {
    _hx_continuation = new ...(..., _hx_completion);
}
_hx_continuation.recursing = true;
```

is therefore unnecessary. For single-state coroutines this is simplified to a direct allocation:

```js
let _hx_continuation = new ...(..., _hx_completion);
```

### 3. TCO suspension inlining in `map_suspension` (`coroFromTexpr.ml`, `coro.ml`)

`map_suspension cb ret e` takes `cb` and `ret` directly; `allow_tco` is computed internally as `(RTailBlock|RTailReturn) && cb.cb_catch = None`. The internal `remap can_tco loop_depth` uses a single `can_tco` predicate that combines "we are in tail position" AND "the outer context allows TCO":

- **`TBlock`**: a custom `remap_block` helper (O(n) single pass) processes all-but-last elements with `can_tco = false` and the last element inheriting `can_tco`.
- **`TIf`** branches inherit `can_tco` from their parent; condition is always `false`.
- **`TSwitch`**: subject is `false`; each `case_expr` and `default` branch inherits `can_tco`.
- **`TTry`**: the try body gets `can_tco = false` (it has this `TTry`'s catch handler); the catch bodies inherit `can_tco` from the outer context.
- **`TWhile`** body and all other sub-expressions are always `can_tco = false`.

A coroutine suspension call is inlined directly (via `make_inline_tail_call`) only when `can_tco` is true. `return suspensionCall()` is handled by detecting the coro call in `TReturn (Some e1)` and delegating to `remap` directly.

The `make_inline_tail_call` callback (passed from `coro.ml` to `expr_to_coro`) produces a complete `texpr` block: `stack_item_inserter + cororesult_var + state_switch`.

## Example

For `@:coroutine function noInternalGoto() { g(); }` (single tail-call), the generated JS goes from:

```js
let _hx_continuation;
if(((_hx_completion) instanceof ...) && _hx_completion.recursing == false) {
    _hx_continuation = _hx_completion;
} else {
    _hx_continuation = new ...(...);
}
_hx_continuation.recursing = true;
let _hx_error = null;
try {
    _hx_loop1: while(true) switch(_hx_continuation.gotoLabel) {
    case 0:
        _hx_continuation.setLocalFuncStackItem(...);
        var _hx_tmp = g(_hx_continuation.completion);
        switch(_hx_tmp.state) {
        case 0: return haxe_coro_SuspensionResult.suspended;
        default: return _hx_tmp;
        }
        break;
    default:
        _hx_error = haxe_Exception.caught("Invalid coroutine state");
        break _hx_loop1;
    }
} catch ...
```

to:

```js
let _hx_continuation = new ...(...);
let _hx_error = null;
try {
    _hx_continuation.setLocalFuncStackItem(...);
    var _hx_tmp = g(_hx_continuation.completion);
    switch(_hx_tmp.state) {
    case 0: return haxe_coro_SuspensionResult.suspended;
    default: return _hx_tmp;
    }
} catch ...
```

`@:coroutine function alsoNoInternalGoto() { if (Math.random() > 0.5) g(); else g(); }` likewise collapses to a single inline if-expression with no dispatch loop, and `@:coroutine function returnCall():Int { return g(); }` is also optimized identically.

On the hxcoro test suite (`build-js.hxml`), this reduces the JS output by ~5 100 lines vs. `development` (57 967 vs 63 055), with all tests passing.

