`OnOptionSelected` started a new delayed coroutine on every click without disabling buttons or cancelling the previous one. Rapid double-clicks could launch overlapping coroutines that advance dialogue cases out of order.

## Changes

- **Immediate button lock** – `OnOptionSelected` sets `interactable = false` on all option buttons before starting the coroutine, blocking further input during the delay window.
- **Coroutine deduplication** – Added `nextCaseCoroutine` field; `SetNextDialogueCaseWithDelay` calls `StopCoroutine` on any in-flight coroutine before starting a new one, ensuring at most one advancement is ever pending.
- **Button restore** – `SetButtons` sets `interactable = true` when the next case's buttons are rendered.

```csharp
private void OnOptionSelected(int optionIndex)
{
    if (currentDialogueCase == null || optionIndex < 0 || optionIndex >= currentDialogueCase.options.Length)
        return;

    // Disable all option buttons immediately to prevent double-click / multiple coroutines
    for (int i = 0; i < optionButtons.Length; i++)
        optionButtons[i].interactable = false;

    String nextSceneIndex = currentDialogueCase.options[optionIndex].nextSceneIndex;
    SetNextDialogueCaseWithDelay(nextSceneIndex);
}

void SetNextDialogueCaseWithDelay(String index, float delaySeconds = 0.5f)
{
    if (nextCaseCoroutine != null)
        StopCoroutine(nextCaseCoroutine);
    nextCaseCoroutine = StartCoroutine(SetNextDialogueCaseDelayedCoroutine(index, delaySeconds));
}
```

